WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:07.808
It's payday lunchtime at our bank, and transfers arrive at 1,600 a second.

2
00:00:07.808 --> 00:00:11.853
The bank's app has a page that shows every balance at once.

3
00:00:11.853 --> 00:00:15.831
And rule 1 says they must always add up to the same total.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.895
Let's shrink the ledger: 50 accounts, with 1,000 cents in each.

5
00:00:20.895 --> 00:00:22.371
So 50,000 in total.

6
00:00:22.667 --> 00:00:25.152
The obvious way to add them up is a walk.

7
00:00:25.152 --> 00:00:27.818
A reader goes through the accounts in order.

8
00:00:27.818 --> 00:00:32.607
It takes one account's lock, reads the balance, adds it, lets go, and moves on.

9
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.170
So here's a question that feels too obvious to ask.

10
00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:40.663
Every read held a lock, and every transfer keeps the books balanced.

11
00:00:40.663 --> 00:00:42.447
So the sum has to be right.

12
00:00:42.447 --> 00:00:43.173
Doesn't it?

13
00:00:43.333 --> 00:00:44.446
Let's trace it.

14
00:00:44.446 --> 00:00:47.414
The reader has already passed account 3.

15
00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:51.865
Now a transfer moves 300 cents from account 40 to account 3.

16
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.120
The reader carries on, and reads the rest, account 40 included.

17
00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.624
What total does it get?

18
00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:01.218
49,700.

19
00:01:01.218 --> 00:01:06.892
It saw account 3 before the money arrived, and account 40 after it left.

20
00:01:06.892 --> 00:01:11.225
Three hundred cents went missing, and nobody took them.

21
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.057
Send the money the other way, and the reader counts the same 300 twice.

22
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.641
Every read held a lock, and no transfer broke a rule.

23
00:01:19.641 --> 00:01:21.783
Still, the sum mixes two moments.

24
00:01:21.783 --> 00:01:26.652
A sum like that is called torn: it matches no state the ledger was ever in.

25
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:34.152
In one Java run of the lesson's demo, 7,241 of 9,456 sums were torn.

26
00:01:34.152 --> 00:01:38.909
A busier machine tore fewer, but never none.

27
00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:41.634
You've seen this on your phone.

28
00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:47.091
A panorama photo sweeps across the room one slice at a time, and every slice is sharp.

29
00:01:47.091 --> 00:01:51.152
But a friend who runs the same way as the camera shows up twice.

30
00:01:51.152 --> 00:01:53.880
And one who walks the other way can vanish.

31
00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:57.998
Great news for the friend who wanted to be in the picture twice.

32
00:01:57.998 --> 00:01:59.349
Less great for a bank.

33
00:01:59.533 --> 00:02:00.381
Let's map it.

34
00:02:00.381 --> 00:02:04.361
The sweep is our reader, going through the accounts in order.

35
00:02:04.361 --> 00:02:06.710
Each sharp slice is one locked read.

36
00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:09.842
And the friend who moves is money in a transfer.

37
00:02:09.842 --> 00:02:15.844
Our 300 cents walked from account 40 back to account 3, against the sweep, so they vanished.

38
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.297
What we want is an ordinary photo: one click, and everyone at the same instant.

39
00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:26.599
For the ledger, one instant means a moment when no transfer is half done.

40
00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:32.434
So the snapshot takes every account's lock, lowest number first, the same order transfers use.

41
00:02:32.434 --> 00:02:35.911
That way, a snapshot can never deadlock with a transfer.

42
00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:37.545
Now no money can move.

43
00:02:37.545 --> 00:02:41.172
A transfer that arrives waits, for a few microseconds.

44
00:02:41.172 --> 00:02:47.285
The snapshot copies all 50 balances into a new record, with the total and a version number.

45
00:02:47.285 --> 00:02:50.173
Once it's built, that record never changes.

46
00:02:50.333 --> 00:02:53.477
Then one write to a field called latest publishes it.

47
00:02:53.477 --> 00:02:57.451
The locks go back, in reverse order, and the waiting transfer runs.

48
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.492
That's the shop's price board from Don't share.

49
00:03:00.492 --> 00:03:03.445
The owner never erases prices while people read.

50
00:03:03.445 --> 00:03:06.644
She prints a new board, and hangs it up in one move.

51
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:08.875
So readers stop adding things up.

52
00:03:08.875 --> 00:03:12.207
They read one field, latest, and take no lock at all.

53
00:03:12.207 --> 00:03:14.974
Reader A read latest just before that write.

54
00:03:14.974 --> 00:03:16.043
What does it see?

55
00:03:19.067 --> 00:03:20.375
All of version 1.

56
00:03:20.375 --> 00:03:21.530
Old, but whole.

57
00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:25.609
Reader B reads just after, and sees all of version 2.

58
00:03:25.609 --> 00:03:27.456
Old or new, never a mix.

59
00:03:28.333 --> 00:03:31.598
Three pictures from the memory model make this safe.

60
00:03:31.598 --> 00:03:38.315
The account locks are the cashier's key: whoever takes the same key next sees everything written before it.

61
00:03:38.315 --> 00:03:44.467
latest is the museum's OPEN/CLOSED sign: a volatile field, whose every read sees the latest write.

62
00:03:44.467 --> 00:03:49.928
And a finished snapshot is a letter in a sealed envelope, whole for anyone who gets it.

63
00:03:50.667 --> 00:03:51.368
Is it free?

64
00:03:51.368 --> 00:03:54.239
Not quite, and here the photo picture breaks.

65
00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:56.280
A camera doesn't stop the party.

66
00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:01.829
Our snapshot does, for a moment, because every transfer waits while it holds the locks.

67
00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.372
For 50 accounts, that's microseconds.

68
00:04:04.372 --> 00:04:07.706
For five million, it's a pause customers would feel.

69
00:04:07.706 --> 00:04:13.668
So a real bank takes one snapshot every few hundred milliseconds, and every reader shares it.

70
00:04:14.467 --> 00:04:16.661
Here it is in Java and Kotlin.

71
00:04:16.661 --> 00:04:18.197
Every lock, in order.

72
00:04:18.197 --> 00:04:20.976
Copy into a record that never changes.

73
00:04:20.976 --> 00:04:22.366
One volatile write.

74
00:04:22.366 --> 00:04:24.121
Then unlock, in reverse.

75
00:04:24.867 --> 00:04:27.347
So, can a sum of locked reads be wrong?

76
00:04:27.347 --> 00:04:32.370
Yes, when each read is locked on its own, like a panorama, one slice at a time.

77
00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:38.220
Build the whole snapshot while no transfer can run, then publish it with one volatile write.

78
00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.365
Quick check.

79
00:04:39.365 --> 00:04:45.719
A reader asks for the latest snapshot in the middle of a transfer from 40 to 3.

80
00:04:45.719 --> 00:04:47.167
Can it get 49,700?

81
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:49.818
No.

82
00:04:49.818 --> 00:04:54.033
Every snapshot was copied while no transfer was half done.

83
00:04:54.033 --> 00:04:57.812
It gets 50,000, maybe from a slightly older version.
